    "# read_transect_nc — Shorescan / TWL Viewer transect processing\n",
    "\n",
    "| | |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| **Author** | Chelsea A. Volpano (cvolpano@gmail.com) |\n",
    "| **Created / last edited** | 2026-11-03 |\n",
    "| **Code assistance** | Claude (configured model: `claude-opus-5-5`) |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Summary (update as the notebook changes)**\n",
    "1. *Transect elevation plot* — plots projected beach elevation vs. local cross-shore distance for every transect of one burst.\n",
    "2. *Batch statistics* — reads every Shorescan timestack netCDF in a folder and writes CSVs per transect + camera, per transect (all cameras), and one combined CSV. Shorescan statistics are computed from the runup **elevation** series `TWL` (zero placeholders removed) and given as elevations (m NAVD88); R2% and setup are heights above the forecast tide + surge, as TWL&CC defines them. The TWL Viewer values stored in the files are corrected for the old +0.082 m datum error, and bursts whose runup sits at the end of the transect are flagged `clipped`.\n",
    "2b. *Quality check* — per camera/transect table and plots of clipped, implausible and plateau values.\n",
    "3. *TWL Viewer vs. Shorescan scatter plots* — one 1:1 plot per transect: forecast TWL against the observed 2% runup elevation (non-clipped bursts).\n",
    "4. *Wave-power intervals* — labels each burst with its cumulative wave-power threshold interval, then plots forecast TWL against the observed 2% runup elevation per interval, with bias and RMSE per interval.\n",
    "5. *Time series* — Hs, forecast TWL vs observed 2% runup elevation, and their difference, with the wave-power intervals shaded in the Section 4 colours.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Coordinate systems.** Cross-shore plots use `localX_transect` (local, shore-normal rotated metres, as written by the Shorescan\n",
    "processing). No reprojection is done here. Shorescan elevations (`elevation_transect`, `TWL`) are taken as m NAVD88.\n",
    "The TWL Viewer values stored in the files (`twl`, `twl05`, `twl95`, `twl_tide`) were 0.082 m too low: the old download applied an LMSL→NAVD88 shift to data already in NAVD88 (Birchler et al., 2024, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coastaleng.2024.104590). Cell 2 adds `DATUM_FIX_M` back.\n",
    "**Time.** File-name timestamps are Unix seconds (UTC). `dt0` is written in US Eastern local time (`America/New_York`, DST-aware);\n",
    "`dt0_utc` is also written so later joins can be done in UTC. `t_mid_utc` is the middle of the capture, from the sample times `T`.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Workflow change log\n",
    "| Date | Change |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
