    "### Profile cleaning (used by Sections 1 and 2)\n",
    "Two artefacts in `elevation_transect`, seen in the Section 1 profiles (2026-11-03):\n",
    "1. **No-data zeros.** At the seaward end the profile drops to about −1 m and then jumps to exactly **0.000 m**. That is not\n",
    "   a measured elevation: it is where the surveyed surface ends and the pixels were filled with 0. It is also off the\n",
    "   profile's elevation grid (the values come in 0.125 m steps offset from zero, e.g. 1.85, 1.975, 2.1 m on c1 t30), and it\n",
    "   is where the 0.000 m runup values in the old CSV came from. A runup line on those pixels is seaward of the survey, so\n",
    "   its true elevation is unknown and lower than the last surveyed point.\n",
    "2. **Spikes.** Single noisy points, e.g. a jump to ~2.1 m near x = 50 m on c2. A runup line that sits on that pixel is\n",
    "   given that elevation, which can produce false plateaus.\n",
    "\n",
    "`clean_profile` sets the zeros to NaN and replaces any point more than `SPIKE_M` from the running median of its\n",
    "`SPIKE_WIN` neighbours by interpolation between its neighbours (only inside the surveyed span; the ends are never\n",
    "extended).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Recomputing TWL.** Shorescan's `TWL` is the profile elevation at the runup pixel `Ri`, so it inherits both artefacts.\n",
    "With `RECOMPUTE_TWL = True`, cell 2 reads `Ri` and looks the elevation up again on the cleaned profile. Before trusting\n",
    "that, it checks that `Ri` really indexes `elevation_transect`: the raw-profile lookup must reproduce Shorescan's `TWL`\n",
    "to within `RI_MATCH_TOL` (median over the burst). If it doesn't, that file keeps Shorescan's `TWL` (zeros still masked)\n",
    "and is counted in the printout, so the method never silently changes the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Coding note.** The running median is one `pandas.Series.rolling(...).median()` call, and the lookup at all 1200 runup\n",
    "positions is one `np.interp` call, so a profile and a burst take well under a millisecond."
   ]
  },
