    "## 2. Batch statistics → CSV\n",
    "Reads every `.nc` in `batch_nc_dir`, computes one row of statistics per file (= one burst at one transect), and writes:\n",
    "* one CSV per transect + camera,\n",
    "* one CSV per transect with all cameras,\n",
    "* one combined CSV.\n",
    "\n",
    "### What changed on 2026-11-03, and why\n",
    "Checks on `Shorescan_TWL_viewer_clean_subset_1.csv` against the raw TWL Viewer JSON showed:\n",
    "1. **The forecast elevations in the files are 0.082 m too low.** `twl` and `twl_tide` were written from the old download, which\n",
    "   shifted NAVD88 data by an LMSL→NAVD88 VDatum offset. On 2024-12-14, 12:00 UTC the file has 0.608 m where the JSON has 0.69 m.\n",
    "   `DATUM_FIX_M` is added to the columns in `DATUM_FIX_COLS`. Heights (runup, setup, swash, Hs) were not shifted.\n",
    "2. **`twl_tide` is `tideWindSetup`, which already includes wave setup.** TWL&CC defines R2% and setup as heights above tide + surge\n",
    "   *without* setup (Stockdon et al., 2006, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coastaleng.2005.12.005), so the matching reference is\n",
    "   `cc_swl = cc_tide − cc_setup`.\n",
    "3. **`Zrunup` and all `TWLstats_*` exceedance/setup values are heights above `twl_tide`**, not elevations. `TWLstats_2exceedence_peaks`\n",
    "   correlated with the old `ss_ru_2pct` at r ≈ 0.97 and not with the tide. Used as an elevation (as the old `ss_twl_2pct_peaks`\n",
    "   column implied), it has r ≈ 0 with the forecast TWL.\n",
    "4. **Many bursts have `TWL` exactly 0.000 m** (e.g. 37 of 307 bursts on c1 transect 30 had a *maximum* of 0.000 m, all at low tide).\n",
    "   An elevation of exactly zero from a projected DEM is not physical, so these are treated as placeholders where the runup line had\n",
    "   no elevation (e.g. off the seaward end). They pulled means and percentiles toward 0.\n",
    "\n",
    "### How each value is now computed\n",
    "| Column | Meaning | Units |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| `ss_twl_mean/min/max/std` | statistics of `TWL` with zeros removed | m NAVD88 |\n",
    "| `ss_twl_2pct` | 98th percentile of the whole `TWL` series | m NAVD88 |\n",
    "| `ss_twl_2pct_peaks` | 98th percentile of individual runup maxima (Stockdon's R2% definition) | m NAVD88 |\n",
    "| `ss_twl_2pct_peaks_file`, `ss_twl_2pct_file` | Shorescan's `TWLstats_2exceedence_*` + the tide it subtracted | m NAVD88 |\n",
    "| `ss_R2` | `ss_twl_2pct_peaks − cc_swl`, comparable with `cc_ru_2pct` | m |\n",
    "| `ss_setup` | `ss_twl_mean − cc_swl`, comparable with `cc_setup` | m |\n",
    "| `ss_S` | 4 × std of cleaned `TWL` (significant swash), comparable with `cc_swash` | m |\n",
    "| `ss_S_file`, `ss_S_ss`, `ss_S_ig` | Shorescan's spectral swash: total, sea-swell, infragravity (vs `cc_swash_inc`, `cc_swash_ig`) | m |\n",
    "| `ss_Tpeak`, `ss_Tmean`, `ss_setup_file` | Shorescan's swash periods and its setup (above `twl_tide`) | s, m |\n",
    "| `ss_ref_tide` | tide Shorescan subtracted, `median(TWL − Zrunup)`; equals the raw `twl_tide` | m NAVD88 (old datum) |\n",
    "| `cc_*` | forecast terms stored in the file, elevations datum-corrected; `cc_swl` = tide + surge | m NAVD88 / m / s |\n",
    "| `beta06`, `ss_beta` | Shorescan slopes (`TWLstats_betaS2006`, `TWLstats_beta`); many `beta06` < 0.005 look like failed fits | – |\n",
    "| `n_zero`, `n_at_limit`, `frac_bad`, `clipped` | runup samples with no surveyed elevation (seaward of the survey, the old 0.000 m values), samples at the cleaned profile's lowest/highest elevation, their share, and `frac_bad > CLIP_FRAC` | – |\n",
    "| `twl_source`, `ri_match_m` | `\"Ri\"` if the runup elevation was recomputed on the cleaned profile, `\"file\"` if Shorescan's `TWL` was kept; and the check value | –, m |\n",
    "| `n_profile_spikes`, `n_profile_nodata` | points removed from `elevation_transect` as spikes, and no-data zeros | – |\n",
    "| `mode_frac` | share of samples at the burst's most common elevation (high = runup line pinned in place) | – |\n",
    "| `implausible` | `ss_setup` outside `SETUP_RANGE`: mean runup too far above or below tide + surge to be real swash | – |\n",
    "| `excluded` | camera/transect pair listed in `EXCLUDE` | – |\n",
    "| `use` | not clipped, implausible or excluded; the rows used in every plot and statistic below | – |\n",
    "| `t_mid_utc`, `duration_s` | capture mid-time and span from `T` (falls back to `n_samples / SAMPLE_HZ`) | UTC, s |\n",
    "\n",
    "The old `ss_ru_2pct`, `ss_zrunup_mean` and `ss_zrunup_std` are dropped: they were heights above the datum-shifted tide that includes\n",
    "setup, so they matched neither the forecast R2% nor an elevation. `ss_zrunup_std` was identical to `ss_twl_std`.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Assumptions.** `TWL` is m NAVD88. Exact zeros are placeholders (`ZERO_IS_FILL`). Shorescan's spectral values (`ss_S_*`, `ss_T*`) were\n",
    "computed by Shorescan and may still include the zero placeholders; `ss_S` does not. Bursts are flagged, not dropped, when clipped, because\n",
    "removing the clipped samples would bias the remaining statistics high (at the seaward end) or low (at the landward end).\n",
    "\n",
    "### Coding notes\n",
    "**Block A – readers.** `netCDF4` returns a *masked array* when a variable has a `_FillValue`. `np.ma.filled(..., np.nan)` turns masked\n",
    "cells into NaN, so the `nan*` functions skip them. Missing variables return NaN instead of raising, so older files without, e.g.,\n",
    "`twl_igSwash` still work. `read_times_s` handles `T` stored as text, as a character array, or as numbers with `units = \"... since ...\"`,\n",
    "and returns `None` if it can't, so the mid-time falls back to `SAMPLE_HZ`.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Block A – `runup_maxima`.** A swash event runs from one up-crossing of the series mean to the next. The up-crossing indices are found\n",
    "with one vectorised comparison (`~above[:-1] & above[1:]`), and `np.maximum.reduceat` takes the maximum of every event in a single\n",
    "compiled call, so there is no Python loop over the ~80–100 events per burst. The 2% peaks value needs at least `MIN_PEAKS` events.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Block A – `burst_stats`.** Keeping the per-burst maths in one function means it can be tested on a made-up array, and the loop in\n",
    "Block B only does file reading. `np.where(is_zero, np.nan, twl)` removes the zeros for the whole array at once.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Block B – loop over files.** Only the 1-D arrays needed are read, never the 1200 × 1181 × 3 image `I`. Each file gives one `dict`,\n",
    "appended to a list; the DataFrame is built once at the end, which is much faster than growing it row by row.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Block C – vectorised time and QC.** Mid-times, the local-time conversion and the `clipped` flag are computed on whole columns. A row is\n",
    "dropped only if a *required* value is NaN/inf; `beta06` is no longer required (it was, and dropped rows with a failed slope fit). The\n",
    "printed checks confirm the datum fix and that `ss_ref_tide` equals the raw `twl_tide`.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Block D – write CSVs.** `groupby` splits the table and writes each group."
   ]
