    "## 3. TWL Viewer vs. Shorescan 2% runup elevation, per transect\n",
    "For each transect, reads the `ALLCAMERAS` CSV that cell 2 wrote (same `RUN_TAG`) and makes a scatter plot with a regression line\n",
    "per camera plus a 1:1 line. The forecast `twl` is a 2% exceedance level (tide + surge + R2%), so it is compared with the observed 2% runup elevation `ss_twl_2pct_peaks`, not the mean (the mean sits near tide + surge + setup and is lower by roughly the swash). Only bursts with `use = True` are plotted (Section 2b). `pd.to_datetime(..., utc=True)` is needed because `dt0` mixes `-04:00` (EDT) and `-05:00` (EST) offsets.\n",
    "Without it pandas returns plain `object` strings. Dates in the title are converted back to Eastern so they match the CSV.\n",
    "Axes are forced square, so a 1:1 line really sits at 45°.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Statistics on each plot.** A text box gives, for each camera (or interval in Section 4) and for all points\n",
    "together:\n",
    "- **n**: number of bursts.\n",
    "- **r** and **R²**: Pearson correlation and its square; how closely the points follow *a* straight line,\n",
    "  whatever its slope or offset. R² is the share of the observed variance the line explains.\n",
    "- **bias**: mean of forecast − observed (positive = forecast too high).\n",
    "- **RMSE**: typical size of the error, in m.\n",
    "- **fit**: the least-squares line *observed = slope × forecast + intercept*, the same line seaborn draws.\n",
    "  A perfect forecast has slope 1 and intercept 0.\n",
    "\n",
    "No p-values are given: bursts 30 min apart are not independent (same tide and waves), so a standard\n",
    "p-value would be far too small. With hundreds of bursts, any visible r is \"significant\" anyway; bias and\n",
    "RMSE are the more useful numbers here.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Coding note.** `fit_stats` computes every statistic with array operations, and `stats_box` applies it to\n",
    "each group with `groupby(...).apply`, so the same function serves Sections 3 and 4 and the printed tables."
   ]
