    "## 4. TWL Viewer vs. Shorescan, coloured by cumulative wave-power interval\n",
    "Each burst is labelled with the cumulative wave-power interval it falls in, then forecast TWL is compared with the\n",
    "observed **2% runup elevation** (`ss_twl_2pct_peaks`, m NAVD88). As in Section 3, the mean runup elevation is not used:\n",
    "the forecast `twl` is a 2% exceedance level, so the mean would sit about a swash height lower by design. Only bursts with\n",
    "`use = True` are plotted.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What an interval is.** `cumulativeWavePower_nbdc44013.ipynb` sums hourly wave power at NDBC 44013 forward and backward\n",
    "from each survey date and records when the sum first passes 200, 400 and 600 kWh/m. For one survey and one threshold, the\n",
    "interval runs from the backward crossing to the forward crossing, i.e. the time around the survey with less than that much\n",
    "wave energy on each side. Intervals are therefore **nested** (200 ⊂ 400 ⊂ 600 for the same survey).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Two ways to group the bursts (`THRESH_MODE`).**\n",
    "- `\"band\"`: each burst goes in one group, the **smallest** threshold whose interval contains it, so the groups are\n",
    "  0–200, 200–400 and 400–600 kWh/m. The groups don't share bursts, so their statistics are independent. This is what the\n",
    "  Section 5 shading shows.\n",
    "- `\"cumulative\"`: each group holds **every** burst within that threshold's interval, so the groups are 0–200, 0–400 and\n",
    "  0–600 kWh/m and a burst near a survey appears in all three. This shows how skill changes as more energetic conditions\n",
    "  are included, but the groups overlap, so their statistics are not independent and differences between them partly\n",
    "  repeat the same bursts.\n",
    "\n",
    "Either way, the \"all\" line in the stats box counts every used burst once, and bursts beyond the largest interval form an\n",
    "\"outside intervals\" group. If a threshold was not reached within the 21-day search window\n",
    "(`datetime_crossed` empty), that side of the interval is set to the window edge (`THRESH_WINDOW_DAYS`).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Time zone.** The crossing times come straight from the NDBC 44013 file index, and NDBC writes both real-time and\n",
    "historical data **in UTC only** (https://www.ndbc.noaa.gov/faq/measdes.shtml). Neither the threshold notebook nor\n",
    "`waveDataDownloader.ipynb` (which builds the times from NDBC's `YY MM DD hh mm` columns) converts them, so the crossing\n",
    "times are **UTC** (checked 2026-11-03). The survey dates are treated as 00:00 UTC, as in the threshold notebook. Since the\n",
    "2026-11-03 update to that notebook, crossing times are interpolated between buoy samples, to the minute."
   ]
  },
